{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>Your seal and your cord</b> — \"Your signet-ring and your wrapper\": a ring with which you seal, and your garment with which you cover yourself — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 38:18). But it is not reasonable that he would give his garment and walk away naked from her. And how could the garment be called a פתיל (cord)? And how would Scripture say (Genesis 38:25) והפתילים in the plural? And if you would say that the garment is called פתיל on account of the fringes-cords of tzitzit — far be it that Judah would fulfill the commandment of tzitzit and yet treat it with contempt by giving it in promiscuity. Rather, perhaps he had in his hand a small cloth (סודר) with which he would sometimes wrap part of his head, and it is called פתיל because it is short like a cord, and this is what Onkelos (Onkelos on Genesis 38:18) translates as שושיפא. And you will not find that Onkelos translates any שמלה in the Torah as שושיפא; rather, he translates the language of כסות (covering) and מלבוש (garment) in all cases. The sole exception is ופרשו השמלה (Deuteronomy 22:17), regarding which he says ויפרשון שושיפא, because it is the known cloth (סודר) in the Talmud (Ketubot 10) by which virginity would be verified. And likewise Jonathan ben Uziel (Isaiah 3:22) translates המעטפות as שושיפא — they are small cloths with which they would wrap the head, and the dignitaries would spread them over the turbans and the ornaments on their heads. And this practice persists to this day in the eastern lands. And it is further possible that Judah had a seal in the shape of a lion or some other known figure, as is the way of rulers, and he had in his hand cords (פתילים) bearing that figure with which to write, and the staff (מטה) was in his hand according to the custom of a ruler and taskmaster, as it is written (Ezekiel 19:14), מטה עז שבט למשול (a staff of strength, a scepter for ruling), and it is written (Genesis 49:10), לא יסור שבט מיהודה (the scepter shall not depart from Judah) — and these he placed in her hand."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 38, :notes {0 "Ramban quotes Onkelos's Aramaic translations (עזקתך ושושיפך) in Hebrew characters at the opening; rendered here in English with the Aramaic transliterated at the point where Ramban himself cites the specific Aramaic terms."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:41:52.120892117Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}